::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A student will speak to classmates about using a homework checklist. What is the clearest purpose? {
~A. To describe every assignment from the week#Incorrect. That would list information, but the scenario focuses on a checklist habit.
=B. To explain how a checklist can help classmates manage homework#Correct. This purpose fits the audience and explains what the speaker wants listeners to understand.
~C. To entertain classmates with a story about homework#Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario suggests the purpose is entertainment.
~D. To compare school rules from different classes#Incorrect. The task is about a homework checklist, not comparing rules.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: Audience: school administrators. Topic: adding a tutoring table after school. Which wording best fits the audience? {
~A. We really need this because students are stressed.#Incorrect. This is understandable but too general for an administrator audience.
~B. Tutoring is a thing people probably want.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal, so it weakens the message.
~C. Everybody should just agree to make tutoring happen.#Incorrect. This sounds demanding and does not show audience awareness.
=D. An after-school tutoring table could provide targeted academic support for students who need help before going home.#Correct. This is formal, specific, and appropriate for administrators.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: Topic: reading before bed. Which sentence is a clear perspective statement? {
=A. Reading before bed can help students create a calmer routine before sleep.#Correct. This sentence gives a clear point of view about the topic.
~B. Reading before bed is the topic of my presentation.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and does not state a perspective.
~C. Many books can be read at night.#Incorrect. This is a general detail, not a guiding perspective.
~D. Some people read and some people do not.#Incorrect. This is a vague observation and does not guide a presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L01 Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Scenario: A student is speaking to teachers about student study routines. Which phrase uses academic vocabulary best? {
~A. Getting stuff done faster#Incorrect. The phrase is informal and too vague for a prepared presentation.
~B. Doing school things better#Incorrect. This wording is general and does not communicate a precise idea.
=C. Developing effective study routines#Correct. This phrase uses clear academic vocabulary appropriate for a teacher audience.
~D. Figuring out all the homework stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is informal and unclear.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A speaker says, "My topic is lunch." What is missing? {
~A. A shorter topic#Incorrect. The problem is not that the topic is too long; it is too unfocused.
=B. A clear purpose and perspective#Correct. The sentence names a topic but does not explain what the speaker will do or what point the speaker will make.
~C. A list of all audience members#Incorrect. Knowing the audience helps, but the immediate problem is lack of purpose and perspective.
~D. A direct quote from a source#Incorrect. Evidence may come later, but the topic first needs a clear direction.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: A student will speak to younger students about staying organized. Which audience detail is most useful for planning? {
~A. The room has a projector.#Incorrect. The room may affect delivery, but it does not describe the listeners' needs.
~B. The presentation happens on Tuesday.#Incorrect. The date is not as useful as knowing the audience's experience.
~C. The speaker owns a planner.#Incorrect. This is about the speaker, not the audience.
=D. The listeners are new to managing several class assignments.#Correct. This detail helps the speaker choose examples and language that fit the audience.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: Purpose: persuade classmates to try a school club. Which opening best matches the purpose? {
=A. Joining one school club can help you meet people and feel more connected at school.#Correct. This opening gives a persuasive perspective that fits classmates.
~B. There are school clubs at many schools.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not persuade classmates to try one.
~C. My presentation includes three slides.#Incorrect. This describes the presentation format, not the persuasive purpose.
~D. Clubs are listed on the school website.#Incorrect. This detail may be useful later, but it does not state a persuasive point.
}

::MLA.ENG1.L.01 U06 L01 Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Scenario: A speaker's note says, "Students needs clear routines when they starts high school." Which revision is correct for standard English? {
~A. Students needs clear routines when they start high school.#Incorrect. The verb "needs" still does not agree with the plural subject "Students."
~B. Student need clear routines when they starts high school.#Incorrect. This version creates number and agreement problems.
=C. Students need clear routines when they start high school.#Correct. Both verbs agree with their subjects, making the statement clear.
~D. Students needing clear routines when starting high school.#Incorrect. This is a fragment and does not make a complete statement.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A student is presenting to parents about digital calendars. Which perspective is most audience-aware? {
=B. Digital calendars can help families support students by making due dates visible at home.#Correct. This perspective connects the topic to a parent audience.
~A. Digital calendars are better than paper all the time.#Incorrect. This is too absolute and does not focus on the parent audience.
~C. Some students have phones and some do not.#Incorrect. This is a detail but not a focused perspective for parents.
~D. Calendars have boxes for dates.#Incorrect. This is too obvious and does not explain why the audience should care.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: A speaker wants to inform classmates about the steps for joining a club. Which purpose statement is strongest? {
~A. I will tell some things about clubs.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not identify the specific information listeners need.
~B. Clubs are fun after school.#Incorrect. This is more of an opinion than a clear informational purpose.
~C. Students sometimes join clubs.#Incorrect. This is a statement of fact, not a strong purpose.
=D. I will explain the steps classmates can follow to join a school club.#Correct. This purpose is specific, audience-aware, and informational.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: Topic: school announcements. Audience: new students. Which perspective is clearest? {
=A. Listening to school announcements can help new students learn important routines and opportunities.#Correct. This gives a clear point of view that fits the audience.
~B. School announcements happen every day.#Incorrect. This is a detail, not a perspective.
~C. New students are at school.#Incorrect. This does not explain the topic or the speaker's point.
~D. I will talk about announcements.#Incorrect. This names the topic but does not state a perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.10 U06 L01 Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Text: "Students who explain ideas in their own words often notice what they understand and where they need more review." Which paraphrase is best for a speaker? {
~A. Students explain ideas and notice all things.#Incorrect. This is vague and loses important meaning from the original text.
~B. Students should memorize every sentence exactly.#Incorrect. This contradicts the idea of explaining in one's own words.
=C. Restating ideas helps students check their understanding and find gaps.#Correct. This keeps the meaning while using new wording.
~D. Review is unnecessary when students explain ideas.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning because the text says explanation can show where review is needed.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A presentation is for classmates who already know the topic. What should the speaker do? {
~A. Repeat only basic definitions the audience already knows.#Incorrect. That does not respect the audience's existing knowledge.
=B. Use examples and details that extend what the audience already understands.#Correct. Audience awareness means adjusting depth and examples for listeners.
~C. Avoid having a clear perspective.#Incorrect. A clear perspective is still needed, even when the audience knows the topic.
~D. Make the presentation less organized.#Incorrect. Organization remains important for any audience.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: A speaker will recommend that students use the library database. Which audience is most specific? {
~A. People#Incorrect. This audience is too broad to guide language or examples.
~B. Everyone at school#Incorrect. This is still broad and does not show who specifically needs the message.
=C. Ninth-grade students beginning their first research assignment#Correct. This audience is specific enough to shape examples and vocabulary.
~D. Anyone who hears it#Incorrect. This does not help the speaker plan an audience-aware message.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L01 Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: A student is replacing informal wording in a presentation. Which revision is most academic? {
=A. "Useful strategy" instead of "good thing to try"#Correct. "Useful strategy" is precise and appropriate for academic speaking.
~B. "A bunch of stuff" instead of "several resources"#Incorrect. This revision moves from precise to vague language.
~C. "Kinda helps" instead of "supports"#Incorrect. This makes the language less academic and less precise.
~D. "Things we do" instead of "routines"#Incorrect. This weakens the vocabulary and makes the meaning less clear.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Scenario: Which plan best shows purpose, audience, and perspective? {
~A. Topic: attendance. I will talk for one minute.#Incorrect. This includes topic and time but no audience or perspective.
~B. Topic: note-taking. People should listen.#Incorrect. This is too vague and does not clearly identify the audience or perspective.
=C. I will explain to classmates how organized notes can make studying easier.#Correct. This includes purpose, audience, and perspective.
~D. My slides will have blue titles and pictures.#Incorrect. This focuses on visuals, not the communication plan.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A speaker is addressing community volunteers. Which tone is most appropriate? {
=B. Respectful and clear#Correct. Community volunteers need a tone that is polite, understandable, and prepared.
~A. Sarcastic and careless#Incorrect. This tone would weaken trust and distract from the message.
~C. Confusing and rushed#Incorrect. A rushed tone makes it harder for listeners to follow.
~D. Overly casual and vague#Incorrect. Casual language may not fit a community audience or a formal task.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: A student says, "Everyone knows this already." What planning problem does this show? {
~A. The speaker has chosen too many sources.#Incorrect. The issue is not source quantity; it is audience assumptions.
~B. The speaker has used too much standard English.#Incorrect. Standard English is not the problem.
=C. The speaker is assuming audience knowledge without checking it.#Correct. Speakers should consider what listeners know and need.
~D. The speaker has written a clear perspective.#Incorrect. The statement does not show a clear perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: Topic: healthy snacks. Audience: students before practice. Which perspective fits best? {
=A. Choosing a simple snack before practice can help students stay focused and energized.#Correct. This perspective fits the student-athlete audience and gives a clear point.
~B. Snacks are food people eat.#Incorrect. This is too obvious and does not provide a useful perspective.
~C. There are many snacks in the world.#Incorrect. This is broad and not audience-aware.
~D. Practice happens after school.#Incorrect. This is a detail, not a focused perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.L.01 U06 L01 Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Scenario: Which sentence is best for a prepared oral presentation? {
~A. Our routines helps us because they keeps us focused.#Incorrect. The verbs do not agree with the plural subjects.
~B. Routines, they help focus because focus is helped.#Incorrect. This sentence is awkward and unclear.
~C. Routines helping students focus during the week.#Incorrect. This is a fragment, not a complete sentence.
=D. Consistent routines help students stay focused during the week.#Correct. This sentence uses standard English and communicates clearly.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A speaker is presenting to classmates about peer tutoring. Which purpose is strongest? {
=B. To explain how peer tutoring can help students review difficult concepts#Correct. This purpose is specific and fits an oral presentation to classmates.
~A. To say peer tutoring exists#Incorrect. This is too limited and does not explain what listeners should understand.
~C. To read every tutoring schedule aloud#Incorrect. That would overload listeners with details instead of giving a focused purpose.
~D. To avoid giving a clear opinion#Incorrect. A presentation needs a clear perspective or purpose.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Scenario: Which sentence best shows audience awareness for new ninth-grade students? {
=A. When you have several classes for the first time, a weekly checklist can help you track what each teacher expects.#Correct. This sentence connects directly to the audience's likely experience.
~B. Students of all ages have always had school responsibilities.#Incorrect. This is broad and less useful for new ninth-grade students.
~C. I personally like checklists better than everything else.#Incorrect. This focuses on the speaker rather than the audience.
~D. Organization is a word with many meanings.#Incorrect. This does not address the audience's needs.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.10
Scenario: A student writes, "My purpose is to inform, my audience is classmates, and my perspective is that study groups can make review less stressful." What is the best evaluation? {
~A. The plan is missing an audience.#Incorrect. The audience is clearly identified as classmates.
~B. The plan has only a topic and no perspective.#Incorrect. The sentence includes a clear perspective about study groups.
=C. The plan includes purpose, audience, and perspective.#Correct. All three planning parts are present and connected.
~D. The plan should remove the purpose.#Incorrect. The purpose is needed to guide the presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Scenario: Which presentation topic has been turned into the strongest perspective? {
~A. Topic: school breakfast.#Incorrect. This is only a topic and does not state a point of view.
~B. School breakfast happens before classes.#Incorrect. This is a detail, not a perspective.
~C. I will talk about school breakfast.#Incorrect. This names the task but does not provide a focused point.
=D. Eating school breakfast can help students begin the day prepared to learn.#Correct. This is a clear perspective that can guide the presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L01 Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A student has a clear perspective but no audience. What should the student do next? {
~A. Remove the perspective from the plan.#Incorrect. The perspective is useful and should remain.
=B. Identify the listeners so the examples and tone can fit them.#Correct. Audience shapes language, examples, and tone in oral communication.
~C. Add unrelated facts to make the speech longer.#Incorrect. Length does not fix missing audience awareness.
~D. Choose visual colors before planning the message.#Incorrect. Design choices should not replace audience planning.
}
